        "## Overview & Intuition\n",
        "\n",
        "We examine characteristic functions (Fourier transforms) of probability measures $\\mu$ on $\\mathbb{R}^d$. \n",
        "\n",
        "* If $\\mu$ is an **absolutely continuous** probability measure with respect to the Lebesgue measure $\\lambda$, so that $d\\mu = f \\, d\\lambda$ with $f \\in L^1(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, then its Fourier transform $\\hat{f}(\\xi) \\to 0$ as $\\|\\xi\\| \\to \\infty$.\n",
        "* By contrast, if $\\mu$ has **discrete components** (pure point measure), the characteristic function does not decay to zero at infinity.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Characteristic Functions of Selected Distributions\n",
        "\n",
        "Below are four classic characteristic functions representing both continuous and discrete probability distributions:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Uniform Distribution**: The characteristic function of the uniform distribution on $[-1, 1]$ is given by the sinc function:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat{f}(\\xi) = \\frac{\\sin(\\xi)}{\\xi}\n",
        "$$"
      ],
      "id": "ca99201a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
